Z||000000||fierodave||03-21-2000||03:04 AM||||"Fiero dave  I need your help"<P>here you go guys!!!!!!!   just had a guy call me and ask me this<BR> <BR>he has a v-6  4-speed  GT<BR>he had the original trani go bad<BR>and swapped in a m-19 from a 84 also a 4-speed   drove the car about 500 miles and noticed oil on the ground,  not coming from the engine  just the trani,  he replaced the drain plug washer that wasn't it,  put in new axel seals, not it,  then saw it was coming from the breather tube on top of the trani and thought it was just over filled,  drained it, added fresh 5w 30  and took it out for a spin.......same thing again drained it,.... and only added 2 3/4 quarts this time,  .....same thing!!!! he plugged the trani breather and that was the end of it until today!!  he noticed the case split gasket weeping and  not the breather leaking like it was  so he pulled out the breather and  he said air  like a ballon for a few secs came out!<BR>This to me, and now to you is this.<BR>have you heard of this? a trani 4-speed not auto building up that much pressure?   I AM lost here ;~o  I never heard of this!  my next door mechanic never did either!   this guy is ready to pull the trani   anyone have an idea on this,   it has to be somthing simple right!<BR>thanks for the space<BR>FD ||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FST2M6||03-21-2000||08:46 AM||||i had a problem with mine spitting out fluid.. i took the top off the breather leaving just an exposed male nipple. I attached a piece of hose about 18" long to it with a nylon zip tie and K&N breather filter at the other end. Straped the breather and hose to the trunk wall and POOF! no leaking, and no more mess... as for the pressure.. don't have a clue... ||||reg||1
Z||000002||stimpy||03-21-2000||07:21 PM||||"exposed male nipple"?<BR> <BR>Patrick, you should be all over that one with some witty repartee! <BR>Don't let me catch you slippin again!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||03-21-2000||07:40 PM||||Plugging the vent can blow a seal. No question. Plus it would seem to me the V6 would throw some extra heat at the tranny vs a 4 cyl because of the exhaust routing. That could expand the air inside quite a bit.<P>An extended vent tube would be a better fix.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierodave||03-21-2000||08:03 PM||||Ok  I see your point with the heat thing!  BUT  the V-6 trani's would have the same problem then correct!<BR>I don't recall a longer breathing tube or hose on the V-6 standards!<BR>the tube was put on today and no more problems (extended hose on the exposed nipple;~) )  but  it still don't explian the trouble, <BR>Is it a design flaw in the m-19?  I would think the engineers mated the v-6 to that trani long before this swap was learned! hmmmmmm  is that the trade off then?  410 ratio from a 365 thanks for the space.<BR>FD||||reg||1
Z||000005||DRH||03-22-2000||12:38 PM||||Dave,<P>I'm not sure how it would "know" if it had a V6 or L4 in front of it other than slightly more heat from engine. Maybe it would leak in an L4 now too???<P>Could the problem be the breather is defective? I'm guessing here but it seems like it's function would be to separate the oil from the air. Maybe something is wrong with it and it's not doing that any more? <BR>The hose thing replaces the breather. Would putting a new breather on it fix the problem also?<P>David.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||03-22-2000||12:54 PM||||Not to get off topic here(no more than needed). Vent tube??? Where is that? I have never seen a vent tube on my 5-speed getrag, and I have pulled the tranny out twice now. Was this something they omitted later in production(mine is an '88)? Or am I just not very observant?||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||03-22-2000||07:31 PM||||Yes, a pluged vent/breather would also blow the seals. same as intentionally pluging it did. I don't know just how that OEM vent is made. some of them do have a filter in them which could plug. others just have a specific size hole in them which can also get blocked. If it's the filter you need to replace it. if it's the hole then you can usually clean it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierodave||03-22-2000||08:47 PM||||Ok a bit off topic now   the breather is not plugged!  there is no filter the hole going into the trani is not plugged   there are NO LEAKS in the trani at all!!!  the oil come out of the breather tube located next to where the linkage shaft goes into the trani  yellow plastic or nylon looking material  but was probably white new!   it's about 1 1/8 inches long out of the trani and is just a push in affair.  it has a little cover on it that will keep water from dripping in, but lets air out and it will spin on the tube if you want it too!  sort of a mushroom cap over the tube Located nest to the control selector lever 84-86 all 4-speeds  called a ventilator,  part number 8640498 all 5- speeds used them  even the getrag  but there numbers you can look up, all standards used them!<BR>the Ventilator is not plugged if it was, there would be no oil coming out of it!  lol  the question IS  what could cause the excessive pressure in the trani?????   That's what we are looking for here Guys!   <BR>thanks for the space<BR>FD<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000009||WKDFIRO||03-23-2000||01:07 PM||||84 tranny cases are pretty weak to begin with. I suppose they were not built to handle a V6 otherwise GM would have carried them over to save money. We have seen at least 2 so far where the 4 speed tranny from an 84 was hooked up with a V6. The 84 trannys mostly had the 4.10. This gear can be put into a V6 4 speed but on a weak tranny like that it is very possible to do damage with a high gear like that.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||03-23-2000||07:41 PM||||you flushed it.....so the only thing in it is oil and air. <P>air expands pretty rapidly when it heats. the AL tranny case can make the air thermal cycle pretty fast. The increased load from the 6 is likely to meat it more than the 4. Not much but enough as far as air goes to make a dif. <P>The oil is likely being splashed on the vent by the gears. The rapidly heating air is carying it out.<P>Every time the tranny cools the cycle starts over.<P>One posibility is that maybe there is an internal baffle in the tranny that has fallen off. The the sheet steel covers on my ford trannys had such a baffle under the vent hole in them. If there is suposed to be a baffle and it's gone you'd have this problem. Unfortunatly the only way to check is to look at another tranny with the vent taken out or split this tranny.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fierodave||03-23-2000||08:19 PM||||Thanks to all who threw in on this one!  a mystery for sure!<BR>it has a 15" tube on it now and the thing is as dry as a bone ;~)<BR>just looks a bit goofy to the trained Fiero eye is all!  I am going to rip open a m-19 in the near future too,  pontiac says no baffle in the trani  they have no clue,  but would remove and inspect it  $300-$600  lol<BR>I think the tube that drains the burped oil  back into the trani is the fix for now!! <BR>Thanks <BR>FD||||reg||1
